1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,800
I'm Shivam Gupta, and this is KindHandoff. When several

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:07,600
people help someone they love, a change of plans

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,400
creates a new job: finding out who can take

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:15,200
over, and whether they actually agreed. KindHandoff makes that

5
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,000
work visible.

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,800
Meet our fictional household. Maya was going to pack

7
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:28,600
a library bag at two thirty, then provide a

8
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:33,400
ride at three. I tell the simulator: “Maya is

9
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:38,200
unavailable this afternoon.” Both commitments now need replacements. The

10
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:43,000
ride also depends on the bag being packed.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,600
Here is why the suggested plan matters. Jo can

12
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:52,200
enter the house between two and three, but cannot

13
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,800
drive. Dev can drive, but is only available from

14
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,400
two forty-five. The planner checks those constraints and proposes

15
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:06,000
Jo for the bag, Dev for the ride.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,600
I review the proposal before creating offers. They remain

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:15,200
pending until each named helper accepts. Jo accepts her

18
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:19,800
bag task in her own helper view. Dev accepts

19
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:24,400
his ride separately. Every acceptance belongs to the person

20
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:29,000
taking responsibility.

21
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,400
Watch the ride. Even after Dev accepts, it is

22
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,800
still waiting for the bag. Jo records the bag

23
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:42,200
as packed, and the ride becomes ready. That small

24
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:46,600
dependency is the difference between having two names on

25
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:51,000
a calendar and having a workable afternoon.

26
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,200
The updated handoff brief shows what changed and what

27
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:59,400
remains. Dev acknowledges this specific version. If the plan

28
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:03,600
changes again, the record still tells us which version

29
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:07,800
he read. People can see what was agreed, by

30
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:12,000
whom, and when.

31
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Behind this is a real MCP server using the

32
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
November twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-five specification and Streamable HTTP. The

33
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
web language simulator is explicitly deterministic. This demonstration uses

34
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,000
persistent household data, real sign-in, and separate helper invitations;

35
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,000
it does not claim a live Alexa connection.

36
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,600
KindHandoff starts with practical support: bags, rides, meals, and

37
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:44,200
visits. We plan to test a household subscription, with

38
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,800
every helper included. The promise is simple: make the

39
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,400
next step clear, and make responsibility something people actually

40
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,000
accept.
